  "teaching": {
    "task": "# Year 12 Physics M7 V03: Spectra from different sources. How do different sources produce different spectral patterns? (design)",
    "causalExplanation": "A spectroscope spreads light out by wavelength. Inside, a grating or a prism sends each wavelength in a slightly different direction, and how a grating does that is a later video. Visible light is a tiny slice of the electromagnetic spectrum, from about four hundred nanometres for violet to about seven hundred for red. So a spectrum is a map. It shows how much light the source gives out at each wavelength. Start with the filament. It's a coil of tungsten wire, heated by the current to about twenty-nine hundred kelvin. In a solid, the atoms are packed tightly and interact strongly, so their charges are shaken in every possible way. Remember, accelerating charges radiate. Shake them in every way, and you get every wavelength, with no gaps. That's a continuous spectrum. Heat the filament more, and it gets brighter, and its light shifts towards the blue end. But its smooth spread depends mainly on temperature, not on the metal, which is why it can't identify an element. Now the discharge tube. It holds hydrogen gas at low pressure, with a high voltage across it. Electrons crash into the atoms and give them energy. Here's the key idea. An electron in an atom can only have certain energies, called energy levels. When it drops from a higher level to a lower one, the atom gives out light of one particular wavelength. A bigger drop gives a shorter wavelength. Hydrogen's drops down to the second level give its four bright visible lines, at six hundred and fifty-six, four hundred and eighty-six, four hundred and thirty-four, and four hundred and ten nanometres. In a thin gas the atoms are far apart, so each behaves on its own, and the lines stay sharp.",
    "conversationalApproach": "Here are three light sources, each viewed through a spectroscope. A hot filament gives an unbroken rainbow. Hydrogen gas in a glowing tube gives four bright, thin coloured lines, on black. And sunlight gives a rainbow crossed by dark lines. Make your prediction. Which of these three patterns can't tell you what the source is made of? It's the filament. The unbroken rainbow looks much the same whatever the filament is made of. The lines are where the information is. A spectroscope spreads light out by wavelength. Inside, a grating or a prism sends each wavelength in a slightly different direction, and how a grating does that is a later video. Visible light is a tiny slice of the electromagnetic spectrum, from about four hundred nanometres for violet to about seven hundred for red. So a spectrum is a map. It shows how much light the source gives out at each wavelength. Start with the filament. It's a coil of tungsten wire, heated by the current to about twenty-nine hundred kelvin. In a solid, the atoms are packed tightly and interact strongly, so their charges are shaken in every possible way. Remember, accelerating charges radiate. Shake them in every way, and you get every wavelength, with no gaps. That's a continuous spectrum. Heat the filament more, and it gets brighter, and its light shifts towards the blue end. But its smooth spread depends mainly on temperature, not on the metal, which is why it can't identify an element.",
    "openingDecision": "opens on v02's final frame (see \"Opening frame\"); the chip \"Next: how do different sources produce different spectral patterns?\" becomes the heading; the method icons fade. On \"rainbow\" the filament's strip fills with a continuous 380 to 750 nm spectrum. On \"black\" the discharge tube's strip shows four bright lines at 410.2 (violet), 434.0 (blue-violet), 486.1 (cyan) and 656.3 nm (red) on black; 26 px tag \"four brightest lines\". On \"lines\" the Sun's strip shows a continuous spectrum with dark lines (a schematic selection including 656.3, 589.0 and 589.6, 486.1 nm; tag \"line strengths schematic\"). On \"prediction\" a 3 s `Countdown` beside three chips: \"filament\", \"hydrogen tube\", \"sunlight\". On \"filament\" the first chip ticks. On \"The lines are where the information is\" the two line strips glow briefly; amber hand tag \"information lives in the lines\" with an amber pen bracket round the two line strips; grey hand tag \"same rainbow, any metal\" on the filament strip.",
    "understandingCheck": "Here are three light sources, each viewed through a spectroscope. A hot filament gives an unbroken rainbow. Hydrogen gas in a glowing tube gives four bright, thin coloured lines, on black. And sunlight gives a rainbow crossed by dark lines. Make your prediction. Which of these three patterns can't tell you what the source is made of? It's the filament. The unbroken rainbow looks much the same whatever the filament is made of. The lines are where the information is. Your turn. A gas in a discharge tube gives bright lines at four hundred and forty-seven, five hundred and two, five hundred and eighty-eight, six hundred and sixty-eight and seven hundred and seven nanometres. Use the reference spectra to identify the gas, and explain your reasoning. Pause here to answer. Last one. Light from the full Moon is viewed through a spectroscope. It shows a continuous spectrum crossed by dark lines. Explain the dark lines, and explain why this is not the Moon's own emission spectrum. Pause here to answer.",
    "curriculumScope": "# Year 12 Physics M7 V03: Spectra from different sources. How do different sources produce different spectral patterns? (design)\n\nStatus: Revision 3 (13 Nov 2026): visual-standard top-up (stage list, question-card pictures, givens strips, five-line notes, simulation engines named, root rules R1 to R4), visuals only. Narration unchanged. Needs a Codex error check before plates and voicing. Previously Revision 2 (7 Nov 2026): brief 191 findings resolved and visual upgrade to maths-visual-standard.md.\n\nNarration 6,291 characters, 15 scenes, about 542 s, counted by script with the build parser rules (total = sum of scene strings; see \"Narration length estimate\"). Opens on v02's final frame.\n\nScope: third video of Year 12 Physics Module 7 (The Nature of Light, Physics Stage 6, 2017 syllabus), inquiry question \"What is light?\". Templates as `phys-y12-m7-v01.md`. No pre-design pack exists; the pack work is done here. Site lessons read in full (6 Nov, read-only, through the brief 168 text extracts, hashes checked against the live files): lesson 1 (see `phys-y12-m7-v01.md`; section `map-the-spectrum` used here), `.../module7/lesson01.sa.html`, SHA256 `28881cb201739957186a78903724b2ee23ae40adc2a4abf95f14b0ba2a940e89`; lesson 9 (orient and predict with the cool-gas prediction, warm-up, intentions, vocabulary, Kirchhoff's laws with Figure 1, real spectral sources with Figure 1b, Doppler shifts, the Doppler worked example, starlight inference, line width as density evidence, the simulator and Activity 2, \"Analyse 7 marks\", \"Account 6 marks\", independent practice Q1 to Q5 with model answers, retrieve with the Payne anchor), `C:/Users/kygs/hscscience/subjects/physics/year12/module7/lesson09.sa.html`, SHA256 `3f660175d3288223d868598098ca915dd30e1e6b34ab652a3ab4c5d60492624b` (both recomputed 6 Nov; match the route). Route entry `phys-y12-m7-v03`, sections `map-the-spectrum` (lesson 1), `classify-spectra-with-kirchhoff` and `identify-real-spectral-sources` (lesson 9). Route start: \"Start from the physical case in the learner question; recall only the earlier result needed for it.\" Route stop: \"Collect or compare discharge-tube, reflected-sunlight and incandescent spectra; explain lines, continuous spectra and elemental fingerprints without confusing reflection with a new atomic emission spectrum.\" Next: \"phys-y12-m7-v04: What can stellar spectra tell us beyond chemical identity?\" Practical in the route: \"Primary or secondary spectra investigation with the three named source types\".\n\nOwner rules applied: as v01 (no people, hands, faces or body outlines; Kirchhoff, Fraunhofer and the helium discovery named in words only; the spectroscope is shown on a stand, never held); painted plates for the filament lamp, discharge tube, EHT supply, pocket spectroscope, Sun and Moon; spectra drawn as coded strips at real wavelengths (NIST, see \"Numbers used\"); energy levels coded; a 3 s prediction; \"Pause here to answer.\" with a 10 s countdown; brief feedback; original attempts; optional key notes; Australian spelling; no em dashes. Recalled, not re-taught: v01 (accelerating charges radiate; c = fλ), v02's final frame, Year 11 and Year 10 atomic structure (electrons in atoms, energy).\n\nSyllabus content (Physics Stage 6 (2017), Module 7 The Nature of Light; ledger ids `P7-IQ1-04`, `P7-IQ1-05`), quoted from the ledger:\n\n- `P7-IQ1-04`: \"conduct an investigation to examine a variety of spectra produced by discharge tubes, reflected sunlight or incandescent filaments\"\n- `P7-IQ1-05`: \"investigate how spectroscopy can be used to provide information about:\" \"the identification of elements\"\n\nSource: https://www.nsw.gov.au/sites/default/files/noindex/2025-03/physics-stage-6-syllabus-2017.docx (page 59)\n\n**Boundary:** starts on v02's final frame (filament, discharge tube and Sun feeding three blank spectrum strips with \"?\"). Teaches: a spectroscope spreads light by wavelength; visible light 400 to 700 nm within the EM spectrum; continuous spectra from a hot dense source (tungsten filament, about 2900 K; every wavelength; depends on temperature, not on the element); emission spectra from a hot thin gas (discharge tube; allowed energy levels; one wavelength per drop; hydrogen's four bright visible lines 656, 486, 434, 410 nm); elemental fingerprints (each element its own set; every observed line must match; sodium's pair, neon, helium's discovery in sunlight); absorption spectra (cool sodium vapour in front of a filament removes sodium's two yellow wavelengths, absorbed from the lowest level; re-emitted in all directions); reflected sunlight (the Sun's absorption spectrum, not a new emission spectrum); Kirchhoff's three rules; the investigation and its safety. Stops before stellar temperature, Doppler shifts and line width (v04), E = hf and Bohr's model (later Module 7 and Module 8), gratings' working (v07).\n\n## Decisions\n\n- **Learner question (route wording kept).** \"How do different sources produce different spectral patterns?\" The answer, built in order: what the source is made of and its state decides the pattern: dense and hot gives every wavelength (3); thin and hot gives only its own wavelengths (4, 5); cool and thin in front of a hot source removes those same wavelengths (6); reflection just returns the original pattern (7).\n- **Hydrogen's lines (Revision 2, brief 191).** The four lines drawn (656.3, 486.1, 434.0, 410.2 nm) are hydrogen's four bright visible lines, not an exhaustive set: fainter violet lines near 397.0, 388.9 and 383.5 nm also exist (NIST). Narration says \"four bright\" lines; the strips carry a 26 px tag \"four brightest lines; fainter violet lines not drawn\".\n- **Hook (route practical's three sources).** The three spectra appear side by side from v02's frame. Prediction: \"Which of the three cannot tell you what the source is made of?\" (chips \"filament\", \"hydrogen tube\", \"sunlight\"); answer: the filament.\n- **Energy levels without E = hf (entry knowledge).** Students have not met photons or E = hf in this course order. Scene 4 says: electrons in an atom can only have certain energies (energy levels); dropping from a higher to a lower level gives out light of one particular wavelength; a bigger drop gives a shorter wavelength. The quantitative link (E = hf, Bohr) is named as later work in one clause. Physics check to confirm this is acceptable at this point (open decision 3).\n- **Continuous spectrum cause (site card).** \"Atoms packed closely together and constantly colliding\" (site) becomes: in a dense solid the atoms are crowded and interact strongly, so their charges are shaken in every possible way, giving every wavelength (links to v01's accelerating charges). Temperature, not the element, mainly sets the spread (bluer and brighter when hotter; quantitative Wien's law is v09's). Revision 2: \"mainly\", because a real filament is only close to the blackbody ideal (author note d); the point carried is that a continuum has no element-specific lines.\n- **Real line positions (owner brief).** All lines are drawn at NIST air wavelengths: H 656.3, 486.1, 434.0, 410.2 nm; Na 589.0, 589.6 nm; He 447.1, 471.3, 492.2, 501.6, 587.6, 667.8, 706.5 nm; Ne (strongest visible subset) 585.2, 588.2, 594.5, 614.3, 640.2, 650.7, 692.9, 703.2 nm. Source: NIST Atomic Spectra Database, lines data (https://physics.nist.gov/asd), air wavelengths; the physics check confirms. Colours of the strip follow wavelength; relative line brightness is schematic (labelled).\n- **Fingerprint matching rule (Revision 2 wording).** Every observed line must match a line in the element's reference spectrum; one matching line is not enough. A reference line that is missing from the observed spectrum (often a fainter line) does not by itself rule an element out; an observed line with no partner does. A1 builds this in: 588 nm matches both helium and neon, but the other four observed lines have no neon partner.\n- **Helium surprise.** A yellow line in sunlight at 587.6 nm matched no known element in 1868; the element was named helium (from the Greek for the Sun) and found on Earth in 1895. Named in words; no scientist drawn.\n- **Absorption (site card and \"Account 6 marks\"; Revision 2 example).** The laboratory example is cool sodium vapour in front of the filament, the classic demonstration: cool sodium atoms sit in their lowest level, and sodium's two yellow lines (589.0 and 589.6 nm) start from that level, so the vapour removes exactly those wavelengths. Revision 1's cool hydrogen is withdrawn: hydrogen's visible lines start from level 2, which cold hydrogen barely occupies (author note g). Same wavelengths as emission because the same energy gaps are involved (electron jumps up). Lines are dark, not empty, because absorbed light is re-emitted in all directions (site). The Sun: hot dense layers give the continuous spectrum; cooler gas above absorbs (site Q1(c) wording \"cooler outer layers\", not a single named shell).\n- **Reflected sunlight (route stop).** Scene 7 makes the point explicitly: the Moon, a cloud or white paper reflects sunlight; the spectrum seen is the Sun's absorption spectrum; reflection does not create a new emission spectrum. Crossed in A2: \"the dark lines come from the Moon's atmosphere\" (the Moon has essentially none) and in scene 7: \"moonlight is the Moon's own emission spectrum\". Earth's atmosphere adds a few absorption bands (author note c; not narrated).\n- **Safety (practical).** Never point a spectroscope at the Sun; view sunlight reflected from white paper or a cloud. Discharge tubes run at high voltage and get hot; the supply is switched off before the tube is changed.\n- **Memory aid (scene 8, new; Kirchhoff's rules):** \"Hot and dense: all colours. Hot and thin: bright lines. Cool and thin in front: dark lines, same places.\" See \"Memory aid check\".\n- **Attempts (original).** A1: identify a gas from lines at 447, 502, 588, 668 and 707 nm using a reference card (H, He, Na, Ne). A2: moonlight's spectrum: explain the dark lines and why it is not the Moon's own spectrum. Crossed: A1 \"neon (588 nm matches)\"; A2 \"the dark lines come from the Moon\".\n- **Handoff (route next).** Stars show the same lines, but some patterns are shifted or broadened. Next: \"What can stellar spectra tell us beyond chemical identity?\". See \"Final frame\".\n- **Colour key:** spectrum strips coloured by wavelength on a 380 to 750 nm axis; dark lines black; emission lines bright on black; energy levels navy; electron drops rose; reference cards grey.\n- **Colour key (Revision 2, fixed for every scene).** As above. Every line and continuum is drawn in its true wavelength colour: 410.2 nm violet, 434.0 nm blue-violet, 447 nm blue, 471 nm blue, 486.1 nm cyan (blue-green), 492 and 502 nm cyan-green, 585 to 589.6 nm yellow (sodium's pair yellow-orange), 594 nm orange-yellow, 614 nm orange, 640 and 651 nm red-orange to red, 656.3 and 668 nm red, 693 to 707 nm deep red. The glow tint of a tube is a separate, tagged role colour (\"glow colour is not data\"). Framed answers in a dark-ink box; crossed chips and their hand tags red; ticked chips green. Hand-font tags take the colour of the quantity they mark.\n- **Type sizes (Revision 2, 1080p).** Line labels on strips 30 px; current statement or answer line at least 44 px (feedback rows 44 to 52 px; framed answers 52 px); live readouts (scene 4 drop selector: drop and λ; scene 3 temperature slider: T) about 52 px; supporting lines and chips 30 to 36 px; hand-font tags at least 30 px; question text 36 px; nothing a student must read below 26 px. All text above the bottom 12% of the frame.\n- **Anchor (Revision 2).** Scene 1 already holds the painted filament lamp, discharge tube and Sun plates (v02's final frame). The delivered discharge tube and pocket spectroscope (brief 191, inspected: a capillary tube in a two-post holder with spring contacts; a slim tube spectroscope with a slit, eyepiece and its own small stand; no people or text) carry scenes 2, 4 and 9; `phys-y11-filament-lamp-v1.png` has true alpha (brief 191), so no replacement is needed.\n- **Simulation (Revision 2).** The core live control is scene 4's drop selector: the electron's drop steps level 3, 4, 5 and 6 down to level 2 while live readouts show the drop and the wavelength (656.3, 486.1, 434.0, 410.2 nm) with a drop-size bar growing (relative sizes 1.00, 1.35, 1.51, 1.60; Python) and the line appearing in its own colour. Scene 3's temperature slider (qualitative, readout \"T\" 2000, 2900, 3500 K) and the reference-card matcher (5, 11) are the other live elements.\n- **Stage changes (Revision 2).** Each source type is its own set-up (spectroscope stage, filament stage, discharge stage, reference cards, absorption stage, reflection stage, three rules, investigation bench). The longest single set-up is scene 4 (about 52 s).\n- **Question cards (Revision 2).** Each card arrives with its full text and a picture in one move. On the answer side the card shrinks to the top, a givens strip lists the data, the picture shows the answer, the answer is framed and one classic slip is crossed.\n- **Site sources used:** lesson 1 `map-the-spectrum` (visible band within the EM spectrum, 400 to 700 nm); lesson 9 `classify-spectra-with-kirchhoff` (three laws, Figure 1, the energy-level explanation, Fraunhofer lines, MC), `identify-real-spectral-sources` (filament 2700 to 3000 K, discharge tube, neon colour, reflected sunlight, Figure 1b), Q1, Q4 and their model answers, \"Account 6 marks\" (re-emission in random directions). Not used: Doppler, line width, Hubble, Payne (v04 handles stellar inference).\n- **Site notes (quoted from site lesson 9, read 6 Nov; for Codex to log in `site-corrections-phys-y12.md`):**\n  1. `retrieve-reflect-and-finish`: \"she determined that stars are 73% hydrogen and 25% helium by mass\". Payne's 1925 thesis showed hydrogen and helium are vastly more abundant than other elements; the mass fractions 73 per cent and 25 per cent are modern values, not her result. Proposed fix: \"she showed that hydrogen and helium are by far the most abundant elements in stars (today's values are about 73% and 25% by mass)\".\n  2. `orient-and-predict` vocabulary: \"Absorption spectrum: A continuous spectrum with dark lines at wavelengths where photons have been absorbed by atoms in a cool gas.\" Correct, but the lesson's first prediction box asks \"Where did that specific colour of light go?\" and the answer (\"re-emit it in random directions\") is only in the retrieve section. Not an error; noted for the site owner as a sequencing suggestion only. (Not for the corrections log.)\n  3. `identify-real-spectral-sources`: \"(and reflect off the Moon, a planet, or a mirror, and pass through Earth's atmosphere) ... Cooler gas atoms along the path absorb photons at their characteristic wavelengths\". This implies the reflecting body's gas forms the Fraunhofer lines; the Fraunhofer lines come from the Sun, and Earth's atmosphere adds only a few telluric bands (mainly oxygen and water). Proposed fix: \"The dark Fraunhofer lines are formed in the Sun's cooler outer layers; reflection from the Moon or a mirror does not change them, and Earth's atmosphere adds only a few extra bands (from oxygen and water vapour).\"\n  Checked and correct: Kirchhoff's three laws, hydrogen's four visible lines, the filament temperature range, the neon colour explanation, Q1 and Q4 model answers, the re-emission explanation.\n- **Cause chain taught, in order:** (1) a spectroscope spreads light by wavelength (2); (2) a hot dense solid emits every wavelength because its crowded atoms shake charges in every way (3); (3) atoms in a thin gas emit only wavelengths matching drops between their own energy levels (4); (4) each element's levels differ, so its lines are a fingerprint (5); (5) cool atoms in front of a hot source absorb those same wavelengths (6); (6) reflection returns the Sun's pattern unchanged (7).\n- **Entry knowledge (route):** v02; v01 (accelerating charges radiate; c = fλ); Year 11 waves; atomic structure from earlier science.\n- **Learner-visible vocabulary:** spectrum (light spread out by wavelength, 2); spectroscope (an instrument that spreads light into its spectrum, 2); continuous spectrum (every wavelength, no gaps, 3); discharge tube (a sealed tube of low-pressure gas made to glow by a high voltage, 4); energy level (one of the only energies an electron in an atom can have, 4); emission spectrum (bright lines on a dark background, 4); absorption spectrum (dark lines on a continuous background, 6).\n- **Legacy logic ported:** none. Kit family `m7-spectra-shift` (Balmer lines, emission and absorption; wavelength-linear display) is the candidate support for scenes 4 to 6 and the final frame; its limit (schematic colours; no pressure broadening) is respected.\n- **New explainer motion:** **spectroscope stage** (source plate, coded slit and spectroscope body, a spectrum strip growing from the slit); **spectrum strip** (380 to 750 nm axis, continuous fill, emission lines on black, absorption lines on a continuous fill, a draggable reference card for matching); **energy-ladder** (a coded atom's four or five navy levels, a rose electron dropping and a coloured arrow to the matching line); **filament jostle** (coded lattice of jiggling dots inside an inset of the filament); **reflection inset** (Sun → Moon or white card → spectroscope, same strip); `Countdown`; `QuestionCard`; `Chip` with `cross`; `KeyNotes`; `Heading`.\n- **Board layout:** heading top-left; source and spectroscope at the left; spectrum strips stacked at the right; everything above the bottom 12%.\n- **Author notes (not learner copy):**\n  (a) Wavelengths are air values from NIST ASD (rounded to 0.1 nm): H I 656.28, 486.13, 434.05, 410.17; Na I 588.995, 589.592; He I 447.148, 471.314, 492.193, 501.568, 587.562, 667.815, 706.519; Ne I 585.249, 588.190, 594.483, 614.306, 640.225, 650.653, 692.947, 703.241. Physics check to confirm against the live database.\n  (b) The 1868 line (D3, 587.6 nm) was seen during a solar eclipse (Janssen) and studied by Lockyer; helium was isolated on Earth by Ramsay in 1895. The narration names no person.\n  (c) Telluric O₂ (A band near 759 nm, B band near 687 nm) and H₂O bands appear in ground-based sunlight; the screen does not draw them as Fraunhofer lines of the Sun (the 380 to 750 nm strip shows the B band only if root wants it; default off).\n  (d) Strictly, a continuous spectrum from a hot solid is close to a black-body spectrum; the narration says only that it depends on temperature (v09 develops it).\n  (e) \"Electrons crash into atoms\" (scene 4) describes collisional excitation in the discharge; the high voltage accelerates free electrons and ions.\n  (f) Pronunciations: \"Kirchhoff\" (KEERK-hoff), \"Fraunhofer\" (FROWN-hoffer), \"nanometres\", \"vapour\".\n  (g) Revision 2 (brief 191): visible Balmer absorption needs atoms in level 2; cold hydrogen is almost entirely in level 1, so a cool hydrogen cell in front of a 2900 K filament would not show the four dark lines. Sodium's D lines (588.995 and 589.592 nm) start from the ground state, so cool sodium vapour absorbs them strongly (the classic sodium-flame or sodium-cell demonstration). In stellar atmospheres of several thousand kelvin enough hydrogen is in level 2 for Balmer absorption, which is why the solar and stellar strips (1, 6, 14) can show hydrogen's dark lines. Not narrated beyond \"Cool atoms sit in their lowest energy level\".\n  (h) Fainter hydrogen lines near 397.0, 388.9 and 383.5 nm lie inside the 380 to 750 nm axis; the strips show only the four bright lines and say so.\n- **Open decisions for root:**\n  - **1. Plates.** Closed in Revision 2: `y12p-discharge-tube-v1.png` and `y12p-pocket-spectroscope-v1.png` were delivered in brief 191 and inspected; `phys-y11-filament-lamp-v1.png` has true alpha (brief 191), so the conditional fallback is not needed. Reused: `phys-y11-eht-supply-v1.png`, `phys-y11-sun-v1.png`, `y12p-moon-v1.png` (M5).\n  - **2. Line data** (author note a) and the optional telluric band (author note c).\n  - **3. Energy levels before E = hf** (Decisions): root to confirm the plain-language level explanation is acceptable ahead of later Module 7 and Module 8 treatment.\n  - **4. Optional beats** the learning review may cut: the helium discovery (scene 5); scene 9's investigation detail (could move to a practical video).\nMain question: *A filament, a glowing gas and sunlight give three different patterns through the same spectroscope. How do different sources produce different spectral patterns?*\nEntry knowledge: v01 and v02; electrons in atoms; wavelength and colour.\nStops after: continuous, emission and absorption spectra; fingerprints; reflected sunlight; Kirchhoff's rules; the investigation. No Doppler, width or temperature evidence.\nHandoff: v04, what stellar spectra tell us beyond chemical identity.\n\n## Visual upgrade (Revision 3)\n\nThe Revision 2 visual upgrade is already written into each scene's Visual line (anchor, simulation, colour roles, hand tags, framed answers). This section tops the design up to the checklist used for Year 11 Physics, Biology and Chemistry, and applies root rules R1 to R4 (`../checks/root-acceptance-y11-upgrade-12-nov.md`). Where a scene has an \"Upgrade (Revision 3)\" paragraph, it adds to the Visual and wins wherever the two differ. No narration line changed.\n\n- **Board stages.** (A) last time's frame: the three painted sources and their three strips, scene 1 (about 35 s); (B) the spectroscope stage, scene 2 (about 31 s); (C) the filament stage with its zoom inset, scene 3 (about 43 s); (D) the discharge stage with the drop selector, scene 4 (about 52 s); (E) the reference cards, scene 5 (about 43 s); (F) the absorption stage, scene 6 (about 50 s); (G) the reflection inset with the Sun and Moon, scene 7 (about 29 s); (H) the three rules, scene 8 (about 17 s); (I) the investigation bench, scene 9 (about 32 s); question and feedback cards; the handoff; the notes board. No set-up runs past about 52 s, so no cut was needed.\n- **Simulation engines (`src/explainer/sims/`).** The two gas insets run on the particle box engine (`particle-box.ts`, drawn with `ParticleBoxView`): the hot thin gas in the discharge tube (scene 4) and the cool thin vapour in front of the lamp (scene 6) are boxes with a few small particles far apart, at a high and a low `temperature`. The drop selector in scene 4 is exact (the four hydrogen wavelengths and their energy drops) and stays lesson-coded, as do the spectrum strips. The hot dense filament (scene 3) stays a lesson animation: the engine has no attraction between particles, so it cannot hold a solid together.\n- **Answer frames and mistakes (root rule R1).** Every answer sits in a dark-ink frame with an ink tick. Every mistake takes a slate scribble and a slate hand tag; \"not possible\" takes a slate cross. This overrides any red crossing, strike, chip or tag in the Visual lines and in \"Colour roles\". Red and green keep only their subject meanings.\n- **Solid ink (root rule R2).** The opening frame and the final frame are drawn solid in full ink wherever a line calls part of them \"faint\", \"ghost\", \"pale\", \"dim\" or \"grey\", so both sides of each join match. Text a student must read is never faint: an earlier row that \"dims\" stays solid at a smaller size, and an earlier path that \"stays faint\" is a thin solid line in its role colour.\n- **No coded drawings of real objects.** Apparatus and real objects are painted plates (the `y12p-` set or existing plates), scaled uniformly and never stretched. Exam-style outlines (ground, walls, cliffs, bench edges), vectors, field lines, graphs and circuit symbols stay coded, as root decision 3 for this course records.\n- **No video numbers on screen.** Where a Visual line tags something with a video code, the on-screen words say \"last time\", \"an earlier video\" or \"next time\". Video codes in this document are notes for the builder.\n- **Question cards.** Each attempt card arrives whole (sentences 38 px) with a picture from its first frame. The picture is answer-free: nothing on it can be counted, measured or read off to get the answer. Method aids (ledgers, formula strips, memory cards) are hidden while a countdown runs; every supplied value and condition stays on the card. Each feedback scene keeps a navy givens strip from its first frame.\n- **Notes scene.** Five one-line bullets (95 characters or fewer, 34 px, role colours), the framed memory card beside them, and a notes scene of 45 s in all.\n- **Sources and cards are plates.** The white source in scene 2 is the painted filament lamp. The white card in scenes 7 and 9 is the painted paper sheet (`phys-y11-paper-sheet-flat-v1.png`). The small source pictures in scenes 1 and 8 are small copies of the lamp and discharge tube plates. The inside of the spectroscope stays a labelled diagram.\n\n## Script and shots\n\nCue words in **bold** trigger the visual change. Exact frames are set from the recording's word timings.\n\n"
