on-screen words say \"last time\", \"an earlier video\" or \"next time\". Video codes in this document are notes for the builder.\n- **Question cards.** Each attempt card arrives whole (sentences 38 px) with a picture from its first frame. The picture is answer-free: nothing on it can be counted, measured or read off to get the answer. Method aids (ledgers, formula strips, memory cards) are hidden while a countdown runs; every supplied value and condition stays on the card. Each feedback scene keeps a navy givens strip from its first frame.\n- **Notes scene.** Five one-line bullets (95 characters or fewer, 34 px, role colours), the framed memory card beside them, and a notes scene of 45 s in all.\n- **Sources and cards are plates.** The white source in scene 2 is the painted filament lamp. The white card in scenes 7 and 9 is the painted paper sheet (`phys-y11-paper-sheet-flat-v1.png`). The small source pictures in scenes 1 and 8 are small copies of the lamp and discharge tube plates. The inside of the spectroscope stays a labelled diagram.\n\n## Script and shots\n\nCue words in **bold** trigger the visual change. Exact frames are set from the recording's word timings.\n\n",
    "transitionPlan": "opens on v02's final frame (see \"Opening frame\"); the chip \"Next: how do different sources produce different spectral patterns?\" becomes the heading; the method icons fade. On \"rainbow\" the filament's strip fills with a continuous 380 to 750 nm spectrum. On \"black\" the discharge tube's strip shows four bright lines at 410.2 (violet), 434.0 (blue-violet), 486.1 (cyan) and 656.3 nm (red) on black; 26 px tag \"four brightest lines\". On \"lines\" the Sun's strip shows a continuous spectrum with dark lines (a schematic selection including 656.3, 589.0 and 589.6, 486.1 nm; tag \"line strengths schematic\"). On \"prediction\" a 3 s `Countdown` beside three chips: \"filament\", \"hydrogen tube\", \"sunlight\". On \"filament\" the first chip ticks. On \"The lines are where the information is\" the two line strips glow briefly; amber hand tag \"information lives in the lines\" with an amber pen bracket round the two line strips; grey hand tag \"same rainbow, any metal\" on the filament strip.\nboard change to the spectroscope stage: the pocket spectroscope plate (`y12p-pocket-spectroscope-v1.png`, delivered in brief 191, on its own painted stand, about 500 px wide), its slit facing a coded white source; a coded cut-away shows a grating inside and the beam fanning into colours. On \"different direction\" the fan spreads violet least to red most, as a grating does (schematic); navy hand tag \"each wavelength its own direction\". On \"electromagnetic spectrum\" a small v01 spectrum ribbon appears above; a bracket zooms on the visible band. On \"red\" the axis \"380 to 750 nm\" writes under the strip, ticks at 400, 500, 600, 700 nm. On \"wavelength\" a small brightness-against-wavelength graph traces above the strip, tag \"a spectrum: brightness at each wavelength\"; navy hand tag \"a map\" and a navy pen arrow from the graph down to the strip.\n\nUpgrade (Revision 3): The white source at the slit is the painted filament lamp (`phys-y11-filament-la